Per-frame presentation-phase instrumentation for vo_avfoundation (Pippin-original).

The tvOS VO has no vblank feedback and runs under --video-sync=audio, so mpv
never computes display-fps, estimated-display-fps, vsync-ratio, vsync-jitter,
mistimed-frame-count or vo-delayed-frame-count for it -- all six read as
unavailable. Two cleanly bracketed judder episodes on device produced no
distinguishing signal at all: the timebase rate held at exactly 1.00000, the
pacing counters from 0010 were zero, and the heartbeat cadence was undisturbed.

The untested hypothesis is a beat between the audio clock frames are scheduled
on and the host/display clock the AVSampleBufferDisplayLayer paces from: with
no display-clock awareness, presentation phase would walk slowly against vblank
-- matching the reported "slowly gets worse, then slowly gets better".

This adds the one measurement that would show it. Every non-anchoring frame
already computes the timebase's projected media position at that frame's
presentation host time; the difference against the frame's own PTS is exactly
the audio-vs-host phase error, and it was previously discarded unless it
exceeded the 20ms re-anchor threshold. It is now accumulated and summarised
once per ~5s window alongside the existing "Presentation pacing over ..." line,
in the same style and on the same cadence. Nothing is logged per frame.

Example line:

  Presentation phase over 5.00s: n=120 (120 smp), mean 3.42 ms, median 3.40 ms,
  min 1.10 ms, max 5.80 ms, walk +0.684 ms/s, min pre-enqueue lead 8.20 ms,
  0 already late before enqueue, 0 anchors

Reading it:

  * `walk` is the movement of this window's mean against the previous one,
    per second of playback -- the direct read of a slow phase walk. It is
    divided by the delta between the two windows' sample-time CENTROIDS, not
    by a window duration: a mean is located at its centroid, so the separation
    between two means is not either window's length unless both happen to be
    equal, which a pause or a stalled flip_page breaks.
  * `walk n/a` means the number is not measurable, and is deliberately distinct
    from a measured `+0.000 ms/s`. It prints on the first window, after a seek
    or reconfigure, and whenever either this window or the one it would be
    compared against contained a timebase anchor -- an anchor zeroes the phase,
    so a mean spanning one carries a sawtooth reset that would be read as
    drift. Only anchor-free windows are retained as history.
  * A mean stepping consistently in one direction across successive windows,
    all reporting `0 anchors` and a numeric walk, is the phase walk. A flat
    mean with a wide min/max spread is ordinary scheduling jitter.
  * `n=` is every frame measured; `(N smp)` is how many the median was computed
    from. They are equal at any real frame rate (a 5s window holds 120 frames
    at 24fps, 300 at 60fps, against a 512-sample reservoir). Past the cap the
    reservoir keeps a uniform random sample of the WHOLE window (Algorithm R),
    never the first N -- a first-N buffer would describe only the start of the
    window, which is exactly wrong for a signal that walks. Even sample counts
    average the two middle values.
  * The lead figures are named "pre-enqueue" because they are measured in
    update_media_timebase_for_frame, before the OSD and video samples are
    handed to the display layer. A frame that only becomes late during enqueue
    is not counted.

Phase diagnostics carry their own window bounds and their own anchor count
rather than borrowing 0010's pacing counters: reset_media_timing() discards the
samples on a seek, and the summary must not then be labelled with a window
length and an anchor count that span discarded frames. The reported window is
the span the retained samples actually cover.

